{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014ZpGmUWGBMjCefRphjZj8x","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntiers:\n  best:\n    meaning: \"Cross-architecture fully replicates: HS precision advantage and temporal gains hold on both 32B models with comparable or better effect sizes\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"r1-32b and QwQ-32B both show HS pre-CP FPR substantially below text-based (ratio ≥1.5x)\"\n      - \"Temporal windowing improves coverage on 32B models (+4pp or more)\"\n      - \"Cross-model transfer (exp-003) achieves above-chance performance (>60% acc), suggesting shared safety signal geometry\"\n      - \"GRU instability replicates on 32B (confirming it's a fundamental signal property, not r1-8b artifact)\"\n      - \"Lead time remains unreliable on 32B (median ≤ 0), confirming Claim 3 as principled limitation\"\n    number_ranges:\n      hs_precp_fpr_32b: [0.12, 0.20]\n      text_precp_fpr_32b: [0.25, 0.40]\n      hs_precision_32b: [0.55, 0.68]\n      text_precision_32b: [0.40, 0.52]\n      temporal_coverage_delta_32b: [0.04, 0.10]\n      cross_transfer_acc: [0.62, 0.75]\n      gru_lead_mean_32b: [2.0, 8.0]\n      gru_lead_median_32b: [-1, 1]\n      n_models_validating_claim1: [3, 3]\n    narrative_direction: \"Original framing intact. Three-model validation (r1-8b, r1-32b, QwQ-32B) makes all 3 claims robust. Cross-transfer result adds bonus novelty ('shared safety geometry across architectures'). Paper title emphasizes the systematic evaluation + cross-architecture consistency. Story: hidden states universally encode safety commitment better than text, temporal context universally necessary, pre-textual detection universally unreliable — this is a property of reasoning models, not one model.\"\n\n  median:\n    meaning: \"Partial replication: HS advantage holds on one 32B model but not both, or margins shrink to borderline significance; cross-transfer fails\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"One 32B model (likely r1-32b, same family as r1-8b) replicates HS precision advantage; the other shows weaker or absent advantage\"\n      - \"Temporal gains are smaller on 32B (<4pp) or inconsistent across models\"\n      - \"Cross-model transfer fails (acc <55%), indicating architecture-specific signal geometry\"\n      - \"Safe FPR remains high (>35%) across all models — deployment utility argument weakened further\"\n      - \"Lead time results are noisy: one 32B model shows slightly positive median, other doesn't\"\n    number_ranges:\n      hs_precp_fpr_32b: [0.18, 0.28]\n      text_precp_fpr_32b: [0.22, 0.35]\n      hs_precision_32b: [0.48, 0.58]\n      text_precision_32b: [0.42, 0.53]\n      temporal_coverage_delta_32b: [0.01, 0.05]\n      cross_transfer_acc: [0.50, 0.58]\n      gru_lead_mean_32b: [-1.0, 4.0]\n      gru_lead_median_32b: [-3, 0]\n      n_models_validating_claim1: [2, 2]\n    narrative_direction: \"Soften Claim 1 from 'substantially more precise' to 'consistently more precise within architecture families, with effect size varying across base architectures.' Claim 2 (temporal necessity) likely still holds as it's method-agnostic. Claim 3 unchanged. Drop cross-transfer from contributions; instead frame architecture specificity as a finding ('safety signal geometry is not universal — probes must be architecture-specific'). Restructure: 2-model validation (r1 family) + 1 partial validation (QwQ) + finding about architecture dependency. Weaker but still publishable — empirical study contribution stands, claims are appropriately scoped.\"\n\n  worst:\n    meaning: \"Cross-architecture invalidation: 32B models show no meaningful HS-over-text advantage, suggesting r1-8b result is architecture/scale artifact\"\n    key_assumptions:\n      - \"Neither 32B model shows HS pre-CP FPR advantage over text (ratio <1.2x or text is better)\"\n      - \"Signal distribution differs: 32B models may encode safety in different subspace, or larger models produce less separable safety representations\"\n      - \"Temporal gains absent or reversed on 32B\"\n      - \"Layer sweep on 64-layer models fails to find any layer matching r1-8b layer-14 discriminability\"\n      - \"All methods converge to similar performance regardless of modality on 32B — distinguishing factor disappears with scale\"\n    number_ranges:\n      hs_precp_fpr_32b: [0.25, 0.40]\n      text_precp_fpr_32b: [0.22, 0.38]\n      hs_precision_32b: [0.42, 0.55]\n      text_precision_32b: [0.43, 0.56]\n      temporal_coverage_delta_32b: [-0.02, 0.03]\n      cross_transfer_acc: [0.45, 0.53]\n      best_layer_fisher_32b: [0.5, 4.0]\n      n_models_validating_claim1: [1, 1]\n    narrative_direction: \"Reframe entirely around scale-dependent safety encoding. New angle: 'Safety commitment signals are detectable in hidden states of smaller reasoning models but become diffuse with scale — implications for monitoring architecture.' Claim 1 becomes conditional ('at 8B scale, HS probes dominate; at 32B, the advantage vanishes'). This is still novel and important for the safety community — it means monitoring strategies cannot be developed on small models and assumed to transfer. New Claim 1: 'Scale fundamentally changes safety signal geometry.' Claim 2 (temporal) may survive if text-based temporal still helps on 32B. Claim 3 strengthened ('even easier detection via HS disappears at scale'). Venue: still EMNLP Security — negative/characterization results about monitoring limitations are valuable. Title shift: 'Does Scale Erase Safety Signals? Hidden-State Monitoring Limitations in Large Reasoning Models.'\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12712,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12712},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"